{
  "title": "The Heap Ladder",
  "levels": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "name": "Heap mechanics",
      "sub": "Start with repeated extract-and-reinsert, then implement heapsort.",
      "study": "Implement a binary min-heap and max-heap without a library: peek, push, pop, sift-up, sift-down and bottom-up heapify. Explain why heapify is O(n), while n pushes are O(n log n). For #912 use heapsort, then compare merge sort.",
      "gate": "Trace duplicates, negatives, one item and an empty pop. Explain parent/child indices, why heap order is not full sorting, and O(1) peek / O(log n) push and pop."
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "name": "Top K and custom ordering",
      "sub": "Keep the best k candidates; expose the weakest kept item.",
      "study": "Use a size-k min-heap for k largest and a max-heap for k smallest. Store original indices when order matters; define all tie-breaks. Compare sorting, buckets and quickselect. #414 has a simpler constant-space solution: explain why a heap is unnecessary there.",
      "gate": "Derive O(n log k), test k=1 and k=n, equal priorities and duplicate values. Write an overflow-safe comparator, not subtraction."
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "name": "Changing priorities and design",
      "sub": "Pop the best current choice, change it, and reinsert it.",
      "study": "Separate active entries from the next unused value. In an infinite-set design, prevent duplicate reinsertion. Never mutate an object priority while it remains inside a heap. For #2208 reason about floating-point values.",
      "gate": "Explain what is stored, what the root means and why the next operation uses it. Account for all k operations in time complexity."
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "name": "K-way merge and sorted frontiers",
      "sub": "Keep one frontier candidate from each sorted source.",
      "study": "Store value plus source/position. Advance only the popped source. Prevent duplicate states for generated sequences. For #632 track the current maximum as well as the minimum. Compare binary search on the answer for sorted order-statistic problems.",
      "gate": "Derive O(N log k) for merging N items from k sources. Explain visited-state rules, empty sources and why the heap does not need every pair."
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "name": "Greedy selection and exchange",
      "sub": "Sort by eligibility or a threshold, heap by a second key.",
      "study": "Distinguish unlocked candidates from chosen candidates. For each problem give an exchange argument for removing the worst chosen item or taking the best available one. Some overlap Greedy on purpose: solve again using an explicit heap invariant.",
      "gate": "Prove the greedy choice instead of saying the heap makes it work. Test an empty eligible set, ties and large accumulated sums."
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "name": "Frequency, cooldown and construction",
      "sub": "Choose by remaining count or lexical order without breaking a constraint.",
      "study": "Use a waiting/cooldown queue when a task cannot be reused yet. Preserve temporarily blocked entries. Decide whether the priority is frequency or character order. Compare the mathematical solution for #621.",
      "gate": "Explain why a second choice is needed when the root is blocked. Detect impossible arrangements and avoid infinite loops."
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "name": "Scheduling, events and resource pools",
      "sub": "Sweep time, release completed work, and pick from eligible work.",
      "study": "Separate available and busy heaps where needed. Add every event whose start is at most now; expire invalid entries; jump time instead of simulating idle ticks. Carefully define same-time release and arrival rules.",
      "gate": "Trace simultaneous starts/finishes, idle gaps and tie-breaks. Explain why sort order and heap order differ, and why each event is pushed/popped only a bounded number of times."
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "name": "Two heaps and order statistics",
      "sub": "Maintain partitions with size and ordering invariants.",
      "study": "Keep the lower half in a max-heap and upper half in a min-heap; rebalance after updates. #480 also needs deletion and valid-size counters, so preview the next stage if needed. #1825 is an advanced extension: balanced ordered sets with sums are often cleaner than heaps.",
      "gate": "State both the partition size rule and max(lower) <= min(upper). Test duplicates across the split, odd/even counts and overflow-safe averaging. For #1825 explain three partitions and maintained sums."
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "name": "Lazy deletion, updates and windows",
      "sub": "Keep versions or expiry data and discard stale roots.",
      "study": "Use an authoritative map or index to validate popped entries. Separate logical size from physical size. Lazy heaps can grow with update history: describe periodic rebuilding or an indexed heap if bounded memory is required. Compare the O(n) monotonic deque for #239.",
      "gate": "Demonstrate a stale root and a stale buried entry. Explain amortized cost and actual memory growth, not just heap size at the end."
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "name": "Shortest paths and stateful search",
      "sub": "Use a heap as the frontier ordered by path quality.",
      "study": "Learn adjacency lists and Dijkstra first. Skip outdated distances; relax edges. The path objective can be sum, maximum edge or probability product. #787 needs stops in the state; #2045 tracks two arrival times and traffic waiting.",
      "gate": "Explain why ordinary Dijkstra needs nonnegative edge weights. Distinguish best distance per node from best distance per (node, resource) state. For counts handle equal distances without double-counting stale states."
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "name": "Grid boundaries and spanning trees",
      "sub": "Expand the cheapest boundary, or the cheapest edge to the tree.",
      "study": "For #407 seed all boundaries and carry the effective water level. #2503 sorts queries offline and reuses a frontier. For #1584 implement Prim and compare Kruskal. For #2290 compare heap Dijkstra with the better-fitting 0-1 BFS.",
      "gate": "Explain the difference between Prim key, shortest-path distance and flood boundary height. Give time and space for your graph representation."
    },
    {
      "id": 12,
      "name": "Mixed recognition: no pattern hints",
      "sub": "Read only the statement first. Decide whether, and how, a heap helps.",
      "study": "Do these without opening earlier stage notes. First record brute force, the operation you need to speed up, the proposed invariant and an alternative data structure. Then solve, test and return after 2-3 days for a blank-editor retry.",
      "gate": "Pass only when you can choose the approach, justify it, code it without an editorial, test edge cases and explain complexity. A green completion tick alone is not mastery."
    }
  ],
